GPU hang, hardware-verified on gfx803 (RX 470): any triton kernel with a
data-dependent `while` loop nested inside a `for`-style loop whose trip
count is exactly 1 hangs the GPU on launch, unconditionally. This is a
genuine triton compiler miscompile, NOT gfx803-specific in mechanism (the
buggy code path has nothing arch-specific in it), though it was only
proven with real gfx803 hardware -- no other ROCm GPU was available to
confirm generically. Kept as a triton-source patch here (not filed
upstream from this repo) because that's this repo's scope; upstreaming is
still the right long-term fix if someone with a second GPU wants to.

Found via vLLM's `_topk_topp_kernel` (vllm/v1/sample/ops/topk_topp_triton.py),
which launches `grid=(min(num_compute_units, batch_size),)` and has each
program grid-stride-loop over its assigned rows
(`for row_id in tl.range(pid, BATCH_SIZE, num_programs)`), with a bounded
ternary-search `while` loop per row. On gfx803 this kernel launches, then
the GPU pegs at 100%/boosted clocks and never returns -- dmesg shows a
real hardware `ring gfx timeout` -> BACO reset, not a soft hang. See
rocm-gfx803 repo SESSION_HANDOFF.md section 8 item 2 for the full
debugging saga (killing the host process does not release the GPU; the
comm name is truncated to "VLLM::EngineCor", `pkill -f EngineCore` misses
it).

INVESTIGATION
-------------
Bisection ruled out the algorithm and the GCN3 DPP patch (this directory's
gfx803-dpp-broadcast-warpreduce.patch) as suspects: standalone `tl.min`/
`tl.sum` (N up to 1024), `tl.cumsum` (N up to 2048), the kernel's own
outlier-gather pass (cumsum + masked scatter, full 151936-vocab, tiled),
and the bisection `while` loop itself (standalone, full vocab, multiple k)
all ran correctly with no hang. The SAME bisection loop wrapped in
`for row_id in tl.range(pid, BATCH_SIZE, num_programs):` hangs -- even at
BATCH_SIZE=1, grid=(1,) (a single program, single row, one outer-loop
iteration). Concurrency (grid=2/4/8/32) was ruled out: all hang
identically to grid=1 once the for-wrapper is present. A minimal
reduction -- a trivial `while` loop (5 fixed iterations, no data
dependency at all) nested in the same `tl.range` for-wrapper -- also
hangs, proving the bug is structural, not specific to the real kernel's
complexity.

ROOT CAUSE
----------
IR diffing (`TRITON_KERNEL_DUMP=1` and `MLIR_ENABLE_DUMP=1`) on the
minimal repro pinpoints the exact pass: `TritonAMDFoldTrueCmpI`
(`tritonamdgpu-fold-true-cmpi`, this file's FoldTrueCmpIOp pattern). Right
before this pass the while's condition block reads
`%1 = arith.cmpi eq, %done_5, %c0_i32; scf.condition(%1) ...` (correct,
matches the frontend TTIR). Right after: `scf.condition(%true) ...` -- the
condition computation was folded to a hardcoded constant.

`TritonAMDFoldTrueCmpI` trusts `TritonIntegerRangeAnalysis`'s range
inference for `cmpIIsStaticallyTrue`. That analysis bounds its
loop-carried-argument fixed-point iteration by `getTotalLoopTripCount`,
which multiplies together the trip counts of a loop AND all its
*enclosing* loops (`getEnclosingLoops`). When the while's own body
argument lattice is visited, if the outer `scf.for`'s trip count is
provably 1 (true here: BATCH_SIZE=1, num_programs=1, so `%row_id` ranges
over exactly one value), the combined bound caps how many times the
analysis will propagate the while's own back-edge (the `scf.yield` in its
"after" region) before giving up. In the degenerate outer-trip-count=1
case this appears to cut the while's own condition value off at its
*initial* value before the back-edge update is ever incorporated, so a
comparison against that initial value gets "proven" constant -- when the
loop body genuinely updates it every iteration and the real answer is
data-dependent. (The exact single line inside MLIR's dataflow solver where
the back-edge contribution is dropped was not pinned down further --
would need live debugging with a full triton build, not attempted here.)

WHAT (the fix, deliberately narrow)
------------------------------------
Rather than touching the range-analysis internals (large, general-purpose,
used by other passes too -- higher risk of new bugs), this patches
`FoldTrueCmpIOp::matchAndRewrite` itself: refuse to fold a `cmpi` op that
is the direct operand of its own enclosing `scf.while`'s `scf.condition`,
regardless of what the range analysis claims. This is the ONE place in
the whole pass where a wrong "provably true/false" answer is catastrophic
(true = infinite loop, false = the loop's body -- including any writes it
was supposed to perform -- never runs) rather than merely a missed
optimization. Every other `cmpi` in a kernel is still eligible for
folding.

VERIFICATION (real gfx803 hardware, RX 470, 2026-08-23)
---------------------------------------------------------
- Minimal repro (trivial 5-iteration while nested in tl.range for): ran to
  completion, correct value, no hang.
- Full bisection-loop repro at grid=1 through grid=32, batch=1 through
  batch=8: all correct pivots, no hang (previously hung at every
  grid/batch combination once the for-wrapper was present).
- vLLM's actual `apply_top_k_top_p_triton(logits, k, p)` called directly
  with batch=8, vocab=151936 (the exact call that originally hung):
  completes, correct output shape, GPU returns to idle.
- Full end-to-end vLLM generation with the triton topk/topp path enabled
  (no pytorch-fallback stopgap): coherent output, clean shutdown, GPU
  idle afterward.

